WEBVTT

00:00:01.940 --> 00:00:03.619
Hi, it's Paola from Italy.
嗨，我是来自意大利的宝拉。

00:00:03.619 --> 00:00:09.560
The first time I watched Alma Mater was during the first wave of COVID that came March 2020.
我第一次看《老爸老妈浪漫史》是在2020年3月新冠疫情爆发的第一波期间。

00:00:09.560 --> 00:00:10.593
My name is Josh.
我叫乔什。

00:00:10.593 --> 00:00:14.339
I first watched the show as a college student in New York.
我第一次看这部剧是在纽约读大学的时候。

00:00:14.339 --> 00:00:15.372
My name is Anna.
我叫安娜。

00:00:15.372 --> 00:00:17.568
I am in Los Angeles, planet Earth.
我在地球上的洛杉矶。

00:00:17.568 --> 00:00:27.772
How I met your mother is special because, whether I've had a fantastic day or a f***ing terrible day, watching that show is always the right move for the mid.
《老爸老妈浪漫史》很特别，因为无论是过了美好的一天还是该死糟糕的一天，看这部剧对心情来说总是最正确的选择。

00:00:27.772 --> 00:00:31.969
My friend told me to watch the show and just said, I'm Ted Mosby.
我的朋友叫我去看这部剧，只说了一句：“我是泰德·莫斯伯。”

00:00:31.969 --> 00:00:37.770
he's talking about, and I watched it and I came back to him and I said, mm-hmm, I'm dead mostly.
他谈到了这个，我看了之后回去对他说：“嗯，没错，我基本上就是个死人。”

00:00:37.770 --> 00:00:42.210
You remember, it was hard time with strict lockdowns, isolation.
你还记得，那是伴随着严格封锁和隔离的艰难时期。

00:00:42.210 --> 00:00:51.090
After a very tough day, every night, I watched some of my episodes to, let's say, overcome such difficult times.
在非常辛苦的一天之后，每天晚上，我都会看几集，可以说以此来熬过那些艰难的时光。

00:00:51.090 --> 00:01:04.689
It feels right, regardless, to go spend time with those characters, those candid, stumbling, hilarious, raunchy, relentlessly then selves people, and that gives the same feeling, the same quality of what good friendship is.
不管怎样，花时间去陪伴那些角色——那些坦率、笨拙、搞笑、带点荤段子、始终如一做自己的家伙们——感觉很对，这带有一种美好友谊的同等感受和品质。

00:01:04.689 --> 00:01:12.489
And being between junior and senior year of college, I was terrified of what was coming next and how to make it happen the way I want.
当时正处于大学大三和大四之间，我对接下来会发生什么以及如何按照自己想要的方式实现它感到恐惧。

00:01:12.489 --> 00:01:21.090
Then there was this wonderful show that was all about how the randomness of life is what gets you exactly where you need to be.
然后就有了这部精彩的剧，它讲的正是生活的随性如何将你带到你正需要去的地方。

00:01:21.090 --> 00:01:36.379
I'll be soon in New York City, when I see you.
我很快就会去纽约市，到时候见。

00:01:36.379 --> 00:01:44.250
Please permit me to tell you everything in New York City.
请允许我在纽约市把一切都告诉你。

00:01:44.250 --> 00:01:46.650
Well, hello, hello.
嗯，嗨，嗨。

00:01:46.650 --> 00:01:48.730
This is Josh Radner.
我是乔什·拉德纳。

00:01:48.730 --> 00:01:51.290
I am here with my friend Craig Thomas.
我和我的朋友克雷格·托马斯在这里。

00:01:51.290 --> 00:01:52.650
Hi, Josh.
嗨，乔什。

00:01:52.650 --> 00:01:54.090
Hey, it's great to be here with you.
嘿，很高兴能和你在这里。

00:01:54.090 --> 00:01:58.250
Welcome to the first episode of How We Made Your Mother.
欢迎来到《老爸老妈是怎么拍的》第一集。

00:01:58.250 --> 00:01:59.969
Are we calling it Wimium?
我们管这叫“Wimium”吗？

00:01:59.969 --> 00:02:01.969
I was wondering about that, Hwimium.
我一直在想这个，Hwimium。

00:02:01.969 --> 00:02:02.969
I think it's Hwimium.
我觉得是 Hwimium。

00:02:02.969 --> 00:02:03.969
Does it work?
这行得通吗？

00:02:03.969 --> 00:02:04.969
I think it does.
我觉得行得通。

00:02:06.650 --> 00:02:16.889
So how I make your mother, which is the show under discussion, is a show that ran on CBS from 2005 to 2014, is that right, Craig?
所以《老爸老妈浪漫史》，也就是我们讨论的这部剧，是一部于2005年至2014年在哥伦比亚广播公司（CBS）播出的剧，对吧，克雷格？

00:02:16.889 --> 00:02:18.610
That is correct, nine seasons.
没错，九季。

00:02:18.610 --> 00:02:19.449
Nine seasons.
九季。

00:02:19.449 --> 00:02:31.050
So almost a decade of our lives, I played the eye in how I make your mother, I played the character of Ted Mosby for nine seasons and Craig tell us what you did on the show.
差不多是我们生活中的十年，我在《老爸老妈是怎么拍的》里饰演那个“我”，我演了九季泰德·莫斯伯这个角色，克雷格，跟我们讲讲你在剧里做了什么。

00:02:31.050 --> 00:02:32.969
I was Barney.
我是巴尼。

00:02:32.969 --> 00:02:35.050
No, that's right, I remember that.
不，对，我记起来了。

00:02:35.050 --> 00:02:40.169
I have not aged well if you're watching on the YouTube.
如果你在YouTube上看的话，我会发现自己老得真是不堪入目。

00:02:40.169 --> 00:02:48.810
No, I'm the co-creator of the show, along with my very brilliant writing partner, Carter Bees, who will be a guest on here to discuss future and future episodes.
不，我是该剧的联合创作者，和我非常才华横溢的编剧搭档卡特·贝斯一起，他也会作为嘉宾来到这里讨论未来以及以后的剧集。

00:02:48.810 --> 00:02:54.370
And we were lucky enough to create the show that found our way to the likes of you, Mr. Ratner.
我们非常幸运能创作出这部剧，并能与像你这样的人相遇，拉德纳先生。

00:02:54.370 --> 00:02:55.210
Oh, thanks.
哦，谢谢。

00:02:55.210 --> 00:03:01.009
Well, Craig and I have obviously been friends now for coming on two decades, which is astonishing.
嗯，我和克雷格显然已经做了快二十年的朋友了，这真让人惊讶。

00:03:01.009 --> 00:03:02.810
We both now live in New York.
我们现在都住在纽约。

00:03:02.810 --> 00:03:15.009
About once a year, I would say, we have a habit of grabbing a meal or some drinks and talk inevitably turns to how I made my mother as well as other things that are happening in our lives.
我想大约一年一次，我们习惯一起吃顿饭或喝几杯，话题不可避免地会转到《老爸老妈浪漫史》以及我们生活中发生的其他事情上。

00:03:15.009 --> 00:03:25.490
But I so enjoy talking about how I made my mother with you and unpacking this thing we made that has been the biggest gift and has carried a lot of shadow with it.
但我非常喜欢和你一起谈论《老爸老妈浪漫史》，并剖析我们创作的这个东西，它一直是最大的礼物，同时也伴随着许多阴影。

00:03:25.490 --> 00:03:32.090
It's just like all things is a very complicated thing, but also a very beautiful thing.
就像所有事情一样，这是一件非常复杂的事情，但也是一件非常美好的事情。

00:03:32.090 --> 00:03:36.050
And you're the person I love talking about this show with the most.
而你是我最喜欢一起谈论这部剧的人。

00:03:36.050 --> 00:03:38.849
So when this idea struck me like, you know what?
所以当这个想法击中我时，你知道吗？

00:03:38.849 --> 00:03:46.210
It's actually time for me to turn around and revisit how I met your mother but also watch it a lot of the episodes for the first time.
其实是时候让我回过头来重新审视《老爸老妈浪漫史》了，同时也要把很多集当作第一次看一样来观看。

00:03:46.210 --> 00:03:48.449
I only watched about half the episodes, what it was on.
当年播出的时候，我大概只看过一半左右的剧集。

00:03:48.449 --> 00:03:56.370
So I will be rewatching the show along with you and our listeners, some of whom I assume will be coming to the show for the first time.
所以我将和你还有我们的听众一起重温这部剧，我想其中有些人可能是第一次看这部剧。

00:03:56.370 --> 00:04:01.610
Other ones will have watched it many times and are just looking for the backstage notes here.
另一些人则已经看过了很多遍，只是在寻找幕后花絮。

00:04:01.610 --> 00:04:13.810
But I figured if I'm gonna do this, why don't we put it on the record and actually just walk through the show together and much like the older wiser narrator Ted, which we are now around that age.
但我想既然我要这么做，为什么不把它记录下来，一起通读这部剧，就像那个年长、更睿智的叙述者泰德一样——我们现在也差不多是那个年纪了。

00:04:13.810 --> 00:04:17.889
Let's look back and see who we were, who we are now, where we're going.
让我们回过头来看看我们曾经是谁，现在是谁，我们要去往何方。

00:04:17.889 --> 00:04:21.250
And yeah, what else would you say about what we're doing here?
是的，对于我们在这里做的事，你还有什么想说的吗？

00:04:21.250 --> 00:04:24.610
Well, first of all, I can't believe you only watched half of the episodes, I'm deeply offended.
嗯，首先，我简直不敢相信你只看了半数剧集，我深受打击。

00:04:24.610 --> 00:04:26.050
I think I'm just gonna walk off.
我想我要直接走人了。

00:04:26.050 --> 00:04:27.410
I'm walking off the project.
我不干这个项目了。

00:04:28.569 --> 00:04:34.170
No, the truth is, I haven't rewatched the show door to door since it went off.
不，事实是，自从它完结后，我还没有从头到尾重温过这部剧。

00:04:34.170 --> 00:04:36.610
I've seen episodes, but I haven't watched the door to door.
我看过单集，但没有从头到尾看过。

00:04:36.610 --> 00:04:40.769
And so I think what we're doing here is excavating back into our past.
所以我觉得我们在这里做的是深入挖掘我们的过去。

00:04:40.769 --> 00:04:43.009
It was almost a decade of our lives.
那几乎是我们十年的生活。

00:04:43.009 --> 00:04:46.569
And we're trying to figure out what did we do there?
我们在试图弄清楚，我们当时都做了些什么？

00:04:46.569 --> 00:04:47.730
What happened?
发生了什么？

00:04:47.730 --> 00:04:48.930
We made this thing.
我们创造了这个东西。

00:04:48.930 --> 00:04:50.490
It's like the pineapple episode.
就像菠萝那一集一样。

00:04:50.490 --> 00:04:52.850
You're like, we have to piece it together.
你会觉得，我们必须把它拼凑起来。

00:04:52.850 --> 00:04:54.370
We're doing the pineapple incident.
我们正在经历菠萝事件。

00:04:54.370 --> 00:04:56.810
but for the entire series, what happened?
但对于整个系列来说，到底发生了什么？

00:04:56.810 --> 00:05:12.569
We woke up, we woke up, we were, we were waking up, we're almost 50 or I think you're, you're there and I'm almost there and we're kind of going, wait, we are closer to the age of future Ted, we are closer to 2030 than we were to do then we are to 2005 when the show launched.
我们醒来，我们醒来，我们当时在，我们当时正在醒来，我们快50岁了——或者我觉得你，你已经到了，而我快到了——我们有点像是在想，等等，我们离年老时的泰德的年龄更近了，我们离2030年的距离，比离剧集开播的2005年更近了。

00:05:12.569 --> 00:05:14.089
And it is just, it is strange.
这真的是，真的很奇怪。

00:05:14.089 --> 00:05:16.370
I was thinking about the sort of symmetry of it.
我一直在思考这种对称性。

00:05:16.370 --> 00:05:23.370
We are something like, I think 20 years since the premiere, I think this September will be 20 years since the premiere.
我们离首播差不多有，我想大概20年了，今年9月应该就是首播20周年了。

00:05:23.370 --> 00:05:28.930
We are just past 10 years of the finale airing, a bit of finishing airing.
我们刚过完大结局播出10周年，也就是完结播出10周年左右。

00:05:28.930 --> 00:05:30.490
So there's a lot of round numbers.
所以有很多整数字。

00:05:30.490 --> 00:05:33.089
There's a 20 and there's a 10 and here we are.
有一个20，还有一个10，而我们就在这里。

00:05:33.089 --> 00:05:37.170
And it just seems like a good moment to kind of look back and take stock of it.
这似乎是一个回首过去并盘点一番的好时机。

00:05:37.170 --> 00:05:47.769
Yeah, I, um, another thing that I, when I called you to talk about this, that I really wanted to talk about, I said, I am astonished one at the, the kind of long tail of the show.
对，我，嗯，当我打电话给你谈论这个时，我真的很想谈的另一件事是，我说，我对这部剧的长尾效应感到惊讶。

00:05:47.769 --> 00:05:59.709
You know, a lot of shows go off the air and, and they go off the air, they go away, you know, and maybe they come up randomly in a, I don't know, trivia night or a, you know, remember that a moment will come back to you.
你知道的，很多剧集播完就播完了，它们就消失了，你知道的，也许它们会随机出现在某个，我不知道，琐事问答之夜里，或者，你知道的，某个“还记得吗”的时刻会再次浮现。

00:05:59.709 --> 00:06:02.339
But there's something about how I made Mother One.
但《老爸老妈浪漫史》有些不同。

00:06:02.339 --> 00:06:05.060
It keeps minting new fans.
它一直在不断产生新的粉丝。

00:06:05.060 --> 00:06:11.540
So people that were babies, children, or even in their teens when it was on, have now discovered it.
所以那些在它播出时还是婴儿、儿童甚至正处于青春期的人，现在已经发现了它。

00:06:11.540 --> 00:06:13.300
I also think there's a really sweet thing.
我也觉得有一件非常温馨的事。

00:06:13.300 --> 00:06:15.779
I think parents are showing it to their teenage kids now.
我觉得现在有些父母正在把这部剧放给他们正处青春期的孩子看。

00:06:15.779 --> 00:06:23.139
They're like, this show taught me a lot about being an adult or young adult navigating all these things.
他们会说：“这部剧教会了我很多关于如何成为成年人，或者作为年轻成年人去应对所有这些事情的道理。”

00:06:23.139 --> 00:06:35.060
But also, I'm astonished at a number of things, but one of the ones that strikes me about when I hear from how I made my mother fans, something I hear so consistently that it's not just anecdotal.
但同时，我对很多事情感到惊讶，其中有一点让我印象深刻，当我听到《老爸老妈浪漫史》粉丝的声音时，我经常听到这样一件事，它不仅仅是个例。

00:06:35.060 --> 00:06:53.139
It has to be, there has to be something there, which is that people say I got turned onto the show or I started watching the show when I was in a really hard moment of my life, whether it's, you know, I was going through a divorce or a sickness or my parent had died.
它一定有某种原因，那就是人们说：“我是因为处于人生的艰难时刻才迷上这部剧的，或者开始看这部剧的，”无论是，你知道的，我正经历离婚、生病，或者我的父母去世。

00:06:54.899 --> 00:07:06.680
I was having a personal kind of moment of crisis and there seems to be that this thing I've noticed that how I met your mother is like soul medicine for people going through hard times.
我当时正经历某种个人危机，我注意到《老爸老妈浪漫史》就像是给经历艰难时期的人们准备的心灵良药。

00:07:06.680 --> 00:07:09.240
And I don't know that it was conceived of that.
我不知道它在构思时就是这样的。

00:07:09.240 --> 00:07:13.399
Exactly, I don't think you and Carter sat down in 2004 and said, I know what we'll do.
确切地说，我不认为你和卡特在2004年坐在一起时会说：“我知道我们要怎么做。”

00:07:13.399 --> 00:07:17.360
We'll do a soul panacea for people.
“我们要为人们做一个心灵万灵药。”

00:07:17.360 --> 00:07:20.639
But it seems like that's what it does for people.
但它似乎就是这样对人们起作用的。

00:07:20.639 --> 00:07:25.199
And I hear it so consistently that it strikes me as something worth exploring.
我经常听到这种说法，这让我觉得这是一件值得探讨的事情。

00:07:25.199 --> 00:07:26.199
It's such an honor.
这真的是一种莫大的荣幸。

00:07:26.199 --> 00:07:32.040
People come up to us all the time and to you, Josh, and say the show got them through something really hard.
人们总是来到我们面前，来到你面前，乔什，说这部剧陪他们度过了非常艰难的时期。

00:07:32.040 --> 00:07:38.279
And getting that, or they say I married my wife because we realized we're both how much mother fans.
听到这个，或者他们说：“我和我妻子结婚了，因为我们发现我们俩都是《老爸老妈》的超级粉丝。”

00:07:38.279 --> 00:07:47.040
So they met in an event or something, like it's just the ways the show has been crucial in people's lives, like the opening words from the fans.
所以他们在一个活动上相遇了诸如此类的，这部剧在人们的生活中至关重要的方式，就像粉丝们的开场白一样。

00:07:47.040 --> 00:07:48.959
We have the greatest fans of any fans.
我们拥有所有粉丝中最棒的粉丝。

00:07:48.959 --> 00:07:49.800
How much more the fans are?
《老爸老妈》的粉丝是什么样的？

00:07:49.800 --> 00:07:50.800
Shout out to the fans.
向粉丝们致敬！

00:07:50.800 --> 00:07:51.879
Huge shout out to the fans.
向粉丝们致以崇高的敬意！

00:07:51.879 --> 00:07:54.519
If you were listening to this, you were an amazing person.
如果你在听这个，你就是一个了不起的人。

00:07:54.519 --> 00:08:00.199
And I find such an openness, Josh, to come talk to us about stuff.
乔什，我发现大家都很敞开心扉来跟我们聊这些事情。

00:08:00.199 --> 00:08:01.240
Do you get this?
你有这种感觉吗？

00:08:01.240 --> 00:08:08.720
I get this a little bit because I'm a writer and people I have to go around telling people, I wear a little shirt that says I create a how much mother and just you got in that needy.
我有点能理解这一点，因为我是一个作家，我必须到处告诉别人，我穿一件小衬衫，上面写着我创造了一个多么伟大的母亲，而你却陷入了那种需要中。

00:08:08.720 --> 00:08:11.240
But no one knows, I have a tattooed on my face.
但没有人知道，我把纹身文在了我的脸上。

00:08:11.240 --> 00:08:13.199
You have 10 most of me tattooed on your face.
你的脸上文着关于我的10个最重要的事情。

00:08:13.199 --> 00:08:26.959
And I've been dying for years to ask you, this sort of two-part question, what was it like being Ted when the show was on and blowing up and not being able to escape Ted's face, your face is dead space, although you are not Ted.
我多年来一直渴望问你一个两部分的问题：当剧集热播并爆红时，作为一个无法摆脱泰德面孔的人，那是什么感觉？你的脸就是泰德的脸，尽管你并不是泰德。

00:08:28.160 --> 00:08:33.360
And then, what does that felt like kind of in the long 10 years since it went off the air?
然后，在节目停播后的这漫长的十年里，那种感觉是怎样的？

00:08:33.360 --> 00:08:34.643
What does that felt like?
那感觉到底是怎么样的？

00:08:34.643 --> 00:08:40.289
The reason I thought of that is that you just said people come up to you and they share these things with you.
我想到这一点的原因是，你刚才说人们会来找你，并与你分享这些事情。

00:08:40.289 --> 00:08:41.726
That seems like a real gift.
这看起来像是一份真正的礼物。

00:08:41.726 --> 00:08:45.320
I'm sure there's also like like enormous, weird downsides, don't they?
我敢肯定，其中也伴随着巨大而诡异的负面影响，对吧？

00:08:45.320 --> 00:08:46.159
Oh, yeah.
哦，是的。

00:08:46.159 --> 00:08:47.000
Do it, do it.
来吧，说吧。

00:08:47.000 --> 00:08:47.840
It's such an interesting thing.
这是一件非常有趣的事情。

00:08:47.840 --> 00:08:49.240
Let's do a little tighter episode on being Ted Mosby.
让我们更深入地探讨一下扮演泰德·莫斯比是什么体验。

00:08:49.240 --> 00:08:56.200
I think there's a lot to unpack that I wouldn't be able to unpack here, but I'll do a little bit of it.
我觉得有很多细节需要剖析，我可能无法在这里完全展开，但我会稍微聊一点。

00:08:56.200 --> 00:08:57.039
I will say this.
我这么说吧。

00:08:57.039 --> 00:09:02.120
I remember I was writing liberal arts for the second film I wrote and directed.
我记得当时我正在写《裂缝》，那是我自编自导的第二部电影。

00:09:02.120 --> 00:09:04.240
And it was 2009 or 10.
那是2009年或2010年。

00:09:04.240 --> 00:09:06.080
I think it was 2010.
我想是2010年。

00:09:06.080 --> 00:09:08.779
And I went to Spain for a month.
我去西班牙呆了一个月。

00:09:08.779 --> 00:09:15.700
Kicked around Spain, went to about five or six cities, wrote during the day and kind of just got into trouble at night on some level.
在西班牙到处闲逛，去了大约五六个城市，白天写作，晚上在某种程度上惹点麻烦。

00:09:15.700 --> 00:09:16.860
I mean, it was just really fun.
我的意思是，那真的非常有趣。

00:09:16.860 --> 00:09:20.740
And my friend, Horula, introduced me to all her friends who were living in Spain.
我的朋友霍鲁拉把我介绍给了她在西班牙生活的的所有朋友。

00:09:20.740 --> 00:09:22.419
So I had like these blind friend dates everywhere.
所以我到处都有这种盲目的朋友约会。

00:09:22.419 --> 00:09:26.179
But I would leave my hotel or wherever I was staying.
但我会离开我的酒店或我住的任何地方。

00:09:27.080 --> 00:09:30.039
And I would people, I was just getting recognized in Spain.
人们……我甚至在西班牙都能被认出来。

00:09:30.120 --> 00:09:31.279
And people would want a picture.
人们会想要合影。

00:09:31.279 --> 00:09:36.360
They want to say, hi, but then they'd so say, do you want to come to a flamenco show in a couple hours?
他们想打个招呼，然后接着说：“你想过几个小时来看弗拉门戈表演吗？”

00:09:36.360 --> 00:09:37.840
And I'd say yes.
我就会说好。

00:09:37.840 --> 00:10:10.559
And it was like my face was this like social I don't know like the fact that they were familiar with me gave them permission it like it was just this weird social lubricant that I was I was accepted everywhere I was it one of the things I really loved about it made the world feel much smaller right you know I've always wanted to not I don't want to talk to every person I meet but I do like the feeling that the world is not so big again filled with anonymous, scary people.
就好像我的脸成了一种社交……我不知道，由于他们对我很熟悉，这就给了他们一种许可，它就像是一种奇特的社交润滑剂，我在哪里都被接纳，这是我真的很喜欢的一点，它让世界感觉小多了，对吧，你知道，我一直希望——我不是说我想和遇到的每个人说话，但我确实喜欢那种世界没有那么大的感觉，里面不再充满匿名的、可怕的人。

00:10:10.559 --> 00:10:19.399
Because I find people are, most people are quite lovely once you get to know them, once you get over that, kind of like your strange endeavor, I don't know who you are or where you're from.
因为我发现人们——大多数人一旦你了解了他们，一旦你跨越了那种“我不知道你是谁或你从哪里来”的陌生感，就会非常可爱。

00:10:19.399 --> 00:10:20.559
Yeah.
对。

00:10:20.559 --> 00:10:24.799
So it gave me like a common language with people.
所以这给了我和人们交流的共同语言。

00:10:24.799 --> 00:10:31.360
I think I've told you this, but in Spain, I went to this little town called Daya and I got really sick.
我想我跟你说过这个，但在西班牙，我去了一个叫代亚的小镇，然后我病得很厉害。

00:10:31.360 --> 00:10:38.879
I got food poisoning for the first 24 hours and I crawled out of the place I was staying And I had hooded sweatshirt on that.
前24小时我食物中毒了，我从住的地方爬了出来，当时我穿着一件连帽卫衣。

00:10:38.879 --> 00:10:40.639
I think it was like tied.
我想帽子是系紧的。

00:10:40.639 --> 00:10:46.480
And I was just trying to get like some ginger ale or something and some crackers because I was so, I was such a mess.
我只是想买点姜汁汽水或者什么东西和一些苏打饼干，因为我太惨了，状态一团糟。

00:10:46.480 --> 00:10:50.980
And there were like 10 or 12 like young college students.
那里有大约10到12个年轻的大学生。

00:10:50.980 --> 00:10:54.779
They were hotel management interns from like all over the world.
他们是来自世界各地的酒店管理实习生。

00:10:54.779 --> 00:10:58.019
And they recognized me like through my hoodie with this sickness.
他们透过我的连帽衫认出了我，尽管我病得不轻。

00:10:58.019 --> 00:10:59.700
And they were like, Oh, that's him.
他们当时说：“哦，就是他。”

00:10:59.700 --> 00:11:00.700
It must be vomiting in a bush.
“他肯定在灌木丛里吐呢。”

00:11:00.700 --> 00:11:02.379
Yeah, yeah, exactly.
对，对，没错。

00:11:02.379 --> 00:11:06.139
And they invited me the next night to either come to launch or dinner I forget.
他们邀请我第二天晚上去吃午饭还是晚饭，我记不清了。

00:11:06.139 --> 00:11:12.059
but I ended up hanging out with these hotel management interns for like three or four days and they were incredible.
但我最后和这群酒店管理实习生一起玩了三四天，他们棒极了。

00:11:12.059 --> 00:11:17.830
But this one guy owned the bar and we were in Mallorca and he owned the bar.
但其中有个家伙在马略卡岛经营着一家酒吧，他有一家酒吧。

00:11:17.830 --> 00:11:20.710
To be clear, not the bar that gave you food poisoning.
澄清一下，不是让你食物中毒的那家酒吧。

00:11:20.710 --> 00:11:21.710
No, no, no, he wasn't.
不，不，不，不是他。

00:11:21.710 --> 00:11:23.389
I got food poisoning in another town.
我在另一个城镇食物中毒的。

00:11:23.389 --> 00:11:24.389
He was 23 years old.
他当时23岁。

00:11:24.389 --> 00:11:27.870
His dad had just died and so he inherited this bar.
他的父亲刚刚去世，所以他继承了这家酒吧。

00:11:27.870 --> 00:11:30.429
He had never been off the island of Mallorca.
他从来没离开过马略卡岛。

00:11:31.309 --> 00:11:41.990
One night he hands me a drink and he says, no-can-doze-filled baby doll, and he knew every single line of how I met your mother.
有一天晚上，他递给我一杯饮料，用那种“no-can-doze-filled baby doll”的语调说话，而且他知道《老爸老妈浪漫史》里的每一句台词。

00:11:41.990 --> 00:11:43.134
That's so amazing.
太神奇了。

00:11:43.134 --> 00:11:53.429
And the thing he told me that I thought was so spectacular was he said, how I met your mother has taught me that what's happening now will be my memories one day.
他跟我说的最让我觉得精彩的一件事是，他说，《老爸老妈浪漫史》教会了他一个道理：现在发生的事情终将成为他一天的回忆。

00:11:54.789 --> 00:12:01.393
So make sure that the present moment is great, that you're making good memories for yourself in the present moment, you know.
所以一定要确保当下过得很棒，确保你正在为现在的自己创造美好的回忆，你知道的。

00:12:01.393 --> 00:12:12.433
And then these hotel management interns, for some reason, like, you know, they would, they would have a few drinks in them, but they would tell me like their deepest, darkest secrets, like some shocking stuff.
然后这些酒店管理的实习生，不知为什么，你知道的，他们可能喝了几杯酒，但他们会跟我倾诉他们心底最深、最黑暗的秘密，一些极其震撼的事情。

00:12:12.433 --> 00:12:12.933
Yeah.
是啊。

00:12:12.933 --> 00:12:15.627
And I think there was something about they knew me.
我想这其中有一部分原因是因为他们认识我。

00:12:15.627 --> 00:12:16.948
They felt like they knew.
他们觉得他们认识我。

00:12:16.948 --> 00:12:18.533
They felt comfortable with it.
他们觉得很自在。

00:12:18.533 --> 00:12:19.378
Yeah, that's it.
对，就是这样。

00:12:19.378 --> 00:12:20.751
This is not an invitation.
这不是在发出邀请。

00:12:20.751 --> 00:12:24.608
By the way, if you're hearing this to come up to me, it's about your job.
顺便说一句，如果你听到这个然后跑来找我，那是关于你的工作。

00:12:24.608 --> 00:12:25.770
Tell you really weird.
说真的，感觉很诡异。

00:12:25.770 --> 00:12:27.143
So let's beat your dishes.
所以让我们注意分寸吧。

00:12:27.143 --> 00:12:28.305
Let's have boundaries.
让我们保持边界感。

00:12:28.070 --> 00:12:39.690
But there is something about this, this is the kind of stuff I really love is it gave me like, like access to people in a way that made the world feel friendly.
但这种感觉真的很奇妙，我真的很喜欢这种事，它让我以一种让世界感到友好的方式去接近人们。

00:12:40.169 --> 00:12:42.116
That's a beautiful story.
这是一个美好的故事。

00:12:42.116 --> 00:12:43.207
That is a key.
这真是一把钥匙。

00:12:43.207 --> 00:12:49.670
You have like the skeleton key to let you into doors that would not have been open.
你就像拥有一把万能钥匙，能带你打开那些本来不会向你敞开的门。

00:12:49.670 --> 00:12:54.809
And I wrote you this little ditty to sing to you in New York City.
我为你写了这首小曲，准备在纽约市唱给你听。

00:12:54.809 --> 00:13:08.000
We'll be right back and now back to the show.
我们马上回来，现在回到节目中。

00:13:08.000 --> 00:13:31.500
It Ted is a complicated character, he's a complicated character to have played because he's the eyes and ears, he's the center of the show, he's the person that kind of welcomes the audience in, but he was also his own really flawed, dimensional character and I have found the people who relate to Ted are unbelievably fascinating to me.
泰德是一个复杂的角色，要演好他并不容易，因为他是故事的眼睛和耳朵，他是节目的核心，他是那个把观众迎进门的人，但他同时也是一个有着严重缺陷、多维立体的角色，我发现那些能与泰德产生共鸣的人对我来说有着不可思议的吸引力。

00:13:31.500 --> 00:13:39.100
Just as many women come up to me and say, I'm like Ted as men, which is really incredible.
有很多女性走过来跟我说，我就像泰德一样，男性也是如此，这真的很不可思议。

00:13:39.100 --> 00:13:47.659
But just like people who are worried they feel a little too much, you know, Ted was probably a HSPs at a highly sensitive person.
就像那些担心自己感情过于丰富的人一样，你知道的，泰德可能是一个HSP，也就是高度敏感的人。

00:13:47.659 --> 00:13:50.419
I think that's fair to say.
我觉得这么说很公平。

00:13:50.419 --> 00:13:58.659
And he just had a big heart and it sometimes got in the way of social, you know, what was expected of him.
他只是有一颗博大的心，而这有时会妨碍到社交，你知道的，妨碍到别人对他的期望。

00:13:58.659 --> 00:14:22.759
But I just, you know, it's an honor to have played someone that kind of raw, right, whose nerve endings were so exposed, and it helped me, you know, it helped me become more vulnerable and, you know, disclose parts of myself that maybe I was embarrassed about that I, that you guys were demanding that they be front and center.
但我只是觉得，你知道的，能扮演这样一个如此真实、神经末梢如此暴露的角色是一种荣幸，它帮助了我，你知道的，它帮助我变得更加脆弱，你知道的，让我敢于袒露自己身体的某些部分，也许我曾为此感到尴尬，而你们要求把这些展现得淋漓尽致。

00:14:22.759 --> 00:14:30.039
also like be even at that time, you know, 2005, like to be a publicly vulnerable male.
而且就像在那时候，你知道的，2005年，作为一个公开展现脆弱的男性。

00:14:30.039 --> 00:14:31.100
Yeah.
对。

00:14:31.100 --> 00:14:34.019
It's still carrying risks, you know, how about now, too?
现在依然有风险，你知道的，现在也是如此吗？

00:14:34.019 --> 00:14:43.659
I mean, it's, it carries risks, yeah, but it was like a big grave thing you guys asked of me and I'm, and I'm grateful and I'm glad that it resonates with people and continues to.
我是说，这确实有风险，是的，但这就像是你们向我提出的一个非常重大的请求，我对此心存感激，我也很高兴它能与人们产生共鸣，并且这种共鸣一直在延续。

00:14:43.659 --> 00:14:44.659
And it's, it's funny.
这很有趣。

00:14:44.659 --> 00:14:47.299
But you said about being vulnerable because it was vulnerable to right, too.
但你刚才说关于变得脆弱的话题，因为创作过程本身也很脆弱。

00:14:47.299 --> 00:14:52.700
Carder and I are these like super emo, like sort of we are highly sensitive people.
卡特和我就是那种超级情绪化、有点像高度敏感人群的人。

00:14:52.700 --> 00:14:55.059
We wear our hearts on our sleeves.
我们把心意都写在脸上。

00:14:55.059 --> 00:14:56.620
We have big dreams like Ted.
我们像泰德一样有着宏大的梦想。

00:14:56.620 --> 00:15:02.379
The creation of this show, when we were 30 years old, that was a far-fetched impossible dream to create this show.
这个节目的诞生，在我们30岁的时候，创作这个节目曾是一个遥不可及、不可能实现的梦想。

00:15:02.379 --> 00:15:07.460
That was us Tedmos being all over the place, coming up with some big far-fetched plan that should never have worked out.
那就是我们像泰德·莫斯比那样异想天开，想出一个本不该成功的宏大而离谱的计划。

00:15:07.460 --> 00:15:15.659
But somehow it did demanding that it rain and somehow it worked out not in all the ways we thought it would, which is the same story for Ted.
但不知怎么的它成功了，它要求下雨而不知怎么的它真的奏效了，虽然结果并不全如我们所想，这也是泰德的故事。

00:15:15.659 --> 00:15:24.940
It never goes exactly how you plan, but it becomes beautiful in some different way than you you ever could have imagined that was the show, what's funny is, Carter and I are very much like that.
事情从来不会完全按你的计划进行，但它会以一种你从未想象过的方式变得美丽，这就是这部剧，有趣的是，卡特和我真的就是这样的人。

00:15:24.940 --> 00:15:29.259
Marshall's a really sweet, emotional, open-hearted, warm, softy of a guy.
马歇尔是一个非常甜美、感性、坦诚、温暖、心软的家伙。

00:15:29.259 --> 00:15:37.659
He's basically had one girlfriend who became his wife, and we would get these notes early on sometimes from well-meaning executives.
他基本上只有一个女朋友并最终结为了妻子，而在早期，我们有时会收到一些善意的高管们的反馈意见。

00:15:39.019 --> 00:15:47.779
Early, and before the show was launched, probably before we even cast it, but also maybe early in the run, where we get these notes, the characters were so based on us at first, right?
在早期，节目开播前，可能甚至在我们选角之前，也可能是在开播初期，我们会收到这样的反馈，一开始这些角色就是以我们为原型的，对吧？

00:15:47.779 --> 00:15:57.259
And then it became like creating it with you guys, But if first it really was us, and we would get these notes from executives, it would be the effect of like, these guys are just such fucking whims.
然后它变成了和你们一起创作的过程，但一开始确实就是我们自己，我们会从高管那里得到这样的意见，大意就是“这些家伙简直就是他妈的软蛋”。

00:15:57.259 --> 00:16:00.820
That's how it felt.
感觉就是这样。

00:16:00.820 --> 00:16:09.019
It was just like, we would get notes that to the effect of like, no man would ever, no man would call himself a man.
就好像我们会收到这样的反馈意见，大意是“没有哪个男人会这样做，没有哪个男人会自称……”。

00:16:09.019 --> 00:16:14.019
Self-respecting man would ever behave in this way.
“任何一个有自尊的男人都不会表现成这样。”

00:16:14.539 --> 00:16:18.019
We're like, um, well, that's based on something that I did.
而我们会说：“呃，好吧，那是根据我做过的事情改编的。”

00:16:18.019 --> 00:16:19.940
And it was really, really weird.
这真的，真的非常诡异。

00:16:19.940 --> 00:16:26.460
And I think it was my little glimpse at probably how it felt to you in literally your body, embodying the character.
我想这让我隐约体会到，你当时在身体和情感上融入这个角色时，大概是什么感觉。

00:16:26.460 --> 00:16:27.299
Yeah.
对。

00:16:27.299 --> 00:16:29.419
And you had to sort of carry that.
而你不得不承担这一切。

00:16:29.419 --> 00:16:33.740
The MTV show Jackass, you were doing that with your heart on CBS every Monday night.
就像MTV的节目《蠢蛋搞怪秀》一样，你每周一晚在CBS也是这样赤诚相见。

00:16:33.740 --> 00:16:39.139
You were just like, what happens if we push Ted's heart off a cliff in a shopping cart?
你们就像是：如果把泰德的心脏用购物车推下悬崖，会发生什么？

00:16:39.139 --> 00:16:40.580
Let's see what happens.
我们来看看会发生什么。

00:16:40.580 --> 00:16:45.940
And it's just like there was kind of a stunt and emotional stuntman quality to that.
这有点像特技表演，而且带有一种情感特技演员的特质。

00:16:45.940 --> 00:16:47.620
And you were kind of doing your own stunts.
而你当时差不多就是在亲自完成这些特技。

00:16:47.620 --> 00:17:03.032
yeah, especially when there's so much confusion between, you know, you know, you play a character and especially if it's the first introduction to you, you know, I didn't have a lot of public things, but I mean, I had pilots and series that didn't go anywhere.
是的，尤其是当现实和角色之间有那么多混淆的时候，你知道，你扮演一个角色，特别是如果这是人们对你的第一次认识，你知道的，我当时没有太多公开的亮相，我是说，我拍过一些没能播出的试播集和剧集。

00:17:03.032 --> 00:17:05.581
And so this is where people kind of met me.
所以这是大家认识我的开始。

00:17:05.581 --> 00:17:21.645
So they thought, well, this is who this is, you know, I do remember at the beginning of, I think, second or third season, but I don't remember which one, but no, I was beginning of third season and you guys wanted to really send Ted and Barney out on a lot of adventures.
因此他们会想，哦，这就是他本人的样子，你知道的，我确实记得在第二季或第三季刚开始的时候——具体是哪一季我记不清了，哦不对，是第三季刚开始的时候，你们想让泰德和巴尼去经历更多冒险。

00:17:21.645 --> 00:17:29.292
And you started writing Ted as a little more Barney-ish in terms of like he was open to like one night stands and all this stuff.
你们开始把泰德写得有点像巴尼了，比如他开始能接受一夜情之类的事情。

00:17:29.292 --> 00:17:37.058
And I remember Pam told me that you guys got a note that said, you know, there's a lot of Barney's on TV, but there's only one Ted.
我记得帕姆告诉我，你们收到了一条意见，大意是：电视上有很多个巴尼，但只有一个泰德。

00:17:37.058 --> 00:17:37.947
Let's keep him.
我们留着他吧。

00:17:37.947 --> 00:17:41.029
Let's preserve his his tendness or his most-beeness.
让我们保持他的“泰德特质”或者说他那种本质。

00:17:41.029 --> 00:17:42.029
Yeah, totally.
对，完全是这样。

00:17:42.029 --> 00:17:52.750
And we had a lot of really smart, strong, female voices from this video and the network who really got the emotion by the show, and we're kind of guardians of that.
当时我们有一群非常聪明、坚定且充满力量的女性声音，来自制片方和电视网，她们真正理解这部剧的情感核心，并且成为了这种核心的守护者。

00:17:52.750 --> 00:17:53.910
And that doesn't always happen.
这种情况并不总是会发生。

00:17:53.910 --> 00:17:54.910
And that was really nice.
这真的很棒。

00:17:54.910 --> 00:17:58.309
And it's not like that fit in with every other show on the network at the time.
而且这并不符合当时电视网上其他所有节目的风格。

00:17:58.309 --> 00:18:01.309
And that was preserved and it was what was beautiful about the show.
但这种特质被保留了下来，这也是这部剧的美妙之处。

00:18:01.309 --> 00:18:02.309
And thank God.
谢天谢地。

00:18:02.309 --> 00:18:03.309
Yeah.
是的。

00:18:03.309 --> 00:18:09.349
