00:26:54.140 --> 00:26:55.263
Is that title too catchy?
这个名字是不是太吸引眼球了？

00:26:55.263 --> 00:26:57.420
Because that's just what you get an acronym for.
因为这直接就能缩写成一个首字母缩略词了。

00:26:57.420 --> 00:26:59.951
Well, let's just get an acronym for it and we'll be able to.
好吧，我们干脆给它弄个缩略词，然后就能……

00:26:59.951 --> 00:27:01.259
It's almost as good as Hwimium.
这差不多跟“Hwimium”一样好。

00:27:01.900 --> 00:27:02.457
Hwimium.
Hwimium。
Hwimium。Hwimium。

00:27:02.457 --> 00:27:02.957
Yeah.
对。

00:27:02.957 --> 00:27:03.793
I love that.
我太喜欢了。

00:27:03.793 --> 00:27:09.229
I mean, my way for Becca, who, and I guess I should have said this the sooner.
我是说，我对贝卡的方式……我想我应该早点说这个的。

00:27:09.229 --> 00:27:13.131
The two of us are kind of like Marshall and Lily, right?
我们俩有点像马歇尔和莉莉，对吧？

00:27:12.980 --> 00:27:16.778
It's we met in Wesleyan University, we've been together that whole time.
我们在卫斯理大学相识，从那以后就一直在一起。

00:27:16.778 --> 00:27:19.733
And we were like the old married couple in our late 20s.
我们二十多岁时就像对老夫老妻一样。

00:27:19.733 --> 00:27:25.379
And that was part of the impetus of how I miss mother and the characters, the creation of those characters.
这也是《老爸老妈浪漫史》以及这些角色、这些角色创作的催化剂之一。

00:27:25.379 --> 00:27:28.914
She is so annoyed because she only ever watched every episode once.
她觉得超级烦，因为她每集只看过一次。

00:27:28.914 --> 00:27:31.500
And she said, here's how I watched every episode.
她说，我是这样看每一集的：

00:27:31.500 --> 00:27:32.715
It would be Monday night.
那会是周一晚上。

00:27:32.715 --> 00:27:33.589
I would be airing.
剧集正在播出。

00:27:33.589 --> 00:27:39.373
You'd be sitting next to me, staring at me, if I laughed at the right spots, you would say did we sound mix that wrong?
你会坐在我旁边，盯着我，如果我在正确的地方笑了，你就会说：“我们的混音是不是弄错了？”

00:27:39.373 --> 00:27:46.129
You didn't hear, I think you didn't hear that line because you didn't laugh and she'd be like, I didn't laugh because it wasn't that great.
你没听到……我觉得你没听到那句台词，因为你没笑。而她会说：“我没笑是因为那没那么好笑。”

00:27:46.129 --> 00:27:47.490
It's not, it's not all gold.
又不是……又不是每一句都是黄金。

00:27:47.490 --> 00:27:48.656
It's not all gold, dude.
老兄，又不是每一句都是黄金。

00:27:48.656 --> 00:27:52.301
And I'd ask her, like, is the music too hot there compared to the dialogue?
然后我会问她，比如那里音乐相对于对白是不是太吵了？

00:27:52.301 --> 00:27:54.099
She'd be like, I don't, I don't care.
她会说，我才不……我不在乎。

00:27:54.099 --> 00:27:55.281
I just want to watch it.
我只想看剧。

00:27:55.281 --> 00:27:55.781
Stop it.
闭嘴吧。

00:27:55.781 --> 00:28:01.492
So that was her great, she, she watched for nine years like that and has basically never seen episodes besides that.
所以这就是她的绝佳体验，她就这样看了九年，除此之外基本上没看过别的剧集。

00:28:01.492 --> 00:28:05.726
And she has been asking for years, like can we We died back in, can we do the rewatch?
而且她已经连续多年要求，比如我们能不能重新看一遍？我们能不能开始重温？

00:28:05.619 --> 00:28:08.980
And I don't know, for whatever reason, I just didn't, I didn't do it.
我不知道，不管出于什么原因，我就是没……我没去做。

00:28:08.980 --> 00:28:09.980
I don't know why.
我不知道为什么。

00:28:09.980 --> 00:28:10.980
Maybe I was waiting for a round number.
也许我在等一个整数。

00:28:10.980 --> 00:28:18.619
I was waiting for these 10s and one of these probably- Well, you know, I know this from watching, you know, films I made, like you stopped thinking about the mix 10 years later.
我在等这十周年什么的……好吧，你知道的，我从看自己拍的电影中知道这一点，比如十年后你就不再考虑混音了。

00:28:18.619 --> 00:28:19.619
Right.
对。

00:28:19.619 --> 00:28:21.619
You know, you'll just- The bad joke.
你知道的，你只会……那个烂笑话。

00:28:21.619 --> 00:28:22.619
You're like that joke.
你会觉得那个笑话……

00:28:22.619 --> 00:28:23.619
Yeah.
对。

00:28:23.619 --> 00:28:24.619
That joke could have been funnier.
那个笑话本可以更有趣的。

00:28:24.619 --> 00:28:25.619
And then you see it later and you're like, that was a good joke.
然后你过一段时间再看，就会觉得，那其实是个好笑话。

00:28:25.619 --> 00:28:26.619
What the fuck is I talking about?
我刚才到底在瞎扯什么？

00:28:26.619 --> 00:28:37.009
And this old man, he must did me, fell in love with you, knew you're a city.
这个老头，他一定……爱上了你，知道你是……

00:28:37.009 --> 00:28:39.250
And now, commercials!
现在，进入广告！

00:28:40.049 --> 00:28:44.369
Hi, I'm Alex Goldman, host of the HyperFixed Podcast.
嗨，我是亚历克斯·戈德曼，《HyperFixed》播客的主持人。

00:28:44.369 --> 00:28:48.049
Each week, we take listeners' problems and try to solve them for them.
每周，我们都会接听听众的难题并试着为他们解决。

00:28:48.049 --> 00:28:51.009
Problems like, I'm 30 and I'm scared to drive in New York.
比如这些问题：我30岁了，但很害怕在纽约开车。

00:28:51.009 --> 00:28:54.730
Or, why can't I adjust the volume of my car stereo when I'm in reverse?
或者，为什么我在倒车时不能调节汽车音响的音量？

00:28:54.730 --> 00:28:57.250
We also solve non-car related problems.
我们也解决与汽车无关的问题。

00:28:57.250 --> 00:29:03.319
If you have a problem, not only will we fix it, we'll expose the hidden systems that cause that problem in the first place.
如果你有什么麻烦，我们不仅会帮你解决，还会揭示导致该问题的隐藏系统。

00:29:03.319 --> 00:29:05.799
That's the HyperFixed Podcast from Radio Topia.
这就是来自 Radio Topia 的《HyperFixed》播客。

00:29:05.799 --> 00:29:20.049
Find it wherever you find podcasts or at HyperFixedPod.com.
你可以在任何地方收听播客，或访问 HyperFixedPod.com。

00:29:20.049 --> 00:29:22.430
End of commercials back to show.
广告结束，回到节目。

00:29:22.430 --> 00:29:28.750
But here's a thing that we were starting to unpack in a talk, but we said, ah, let's save it for the show.
但这里有个事，我们刚才在聊天时正要展开说，但我们说：啊，留到节目里说吧。

00:29:28.750 --> 00:29:37.190
And we can also dip into this with Carter, but it's so fascinating to me that you guys were the age of the characters you were writing essentially.
我们也可以跟卡特深入探讨这个，但我感到非常迷人的是，你们写这些角色时，你们的年龄基本上和角色是一样的。

00:29:37.390 --> 00:29:38.230
Yeah.
对。

00:29:38.230 --> 00:29:52.309
And yet it was framed as a flashback from an older wiser man who was offering this kind of wisdom and perspective born of his years of being farther along and being able to look back and contextualize it.
然而它的框架却是一个年长且更明智的男人的回忆录，他提供了这种由岁月沉淀、走得更远并能够回首和将其情境化的智慧与视角。

00:29:52.309 --> 00:30:18.250
And so you were having to, from inside of the experience, you were having to almost beam your consciousness forward 20 or 25 years and reflect back on the moment you were in, which is like a heroic amount to ask, I think, of any one much less, you know, young kids running their first TV show, like, were you conscious of the weirdness of that?
所以你们必须在经历的当下，几乎是将你们的意识向前投射20或25年，去反思你们正身处的那个时刻，我觉得这简直是对任何人——更不用说那些运营自己第一部电视剧的年轻小伙子们——提出了英雄般的要求，你当时意识到这种怪异感了吗？

00:30:18.250 --> 00:30:20.849
It, well, it, it seems weirder now in a way.
它……好吧，从某种程度上来说，现在看起来更怪了。

00:30:20.849 --> 00:30:22.490
It almost didn't seem that weird at the time.
当时倒几乎没觉得那么怪。

00:30:22.490 --> 00:30:26.250
And I, I think it's because we fancied ourselves, older and wiser than our ears.
而且我……我想这是因为我们自诩比实际年龄更成熟、更睿智。

00:30:26.250 --> 00:30:27.250
Right.
对。

00:30:27.250 --> 00:30:30.769
I think I, yeah, we were like, we were like, 32 writing 52 or something, right?
我想我……对，我们当时就像是……32岁的人在写52岁的事之类的好吧？

00:30:30.769 --> 00:30:36.970
When we were writing, thinking about future Ted's perspectives and his, and his mid fifties, whatever he is, we're now far closer to that.
当我们写作时，在考虑未来泰德在五十多岁时的视角，无论他现在多大，我们现在离那个年纪近得多了。

00:30:36.970 --> 00:30:44.740
And I look back at it and I do think it had something to do with wanting, being drawn to the idea of reassurance of the future.
我回过头来看，确实觉得这与对未来的渴望、被未来的安全感这一概念所吸引有关。

00:30:44.740 --> 00:30:48.299
There's a future you, because you feel so lost when you're 30, right?
有一个未来的你，因为当你30岁时你感到如此迷茫，对吧？

00:30:48.299 --> 00:30:51.019
You just, you're not, you're not actually quite an adult, right?
你只是……你其实还算不上是一个真正的成年人，对吧？

00:30:51.019 --> 00:30:56.220
We were, we all felt like fraud, so we were all the same age, we're all these little kids.
我们都觉得自己像骗子，我们当时都一样大，都是这些小屁孩。

00:30:56.220 --> 00:31:00.140
We somehow fooled our way into having a TV show.
我们不知怎么地糊里糊涂地搞到了一部电视剧。

00:31:00.140 --> 00:31:11.579
And like we, I think we were just imagining our future selves telling us what we wanted to here now, in this moment, because we felt lost, because we felt scared, because we felt haunted.
而且我觉得我们当时只是在想象未来的自己在告诉我们此刻、此时我们最想听到的话，因为我们感到迷茫，因为我们感到害怕，因为我们感到心神不定。

00:31:11.579 --> 00:31:15.259
And that idea of that future Ted gave us so much confidence.
而未来泰德的那个概念给了我们极大的信心。

00:31:15.259 --> 00:31:18.859
Like it just, it sort of lifted us up to be looking through that lens.
它就像是……通过那种视角来看待事物，把我们振作了起来。

00:31:18.859 --> 00:31:22.900
And we, by, by pretending to be that thing, we sort of became that thing.
而且我们通过假装成为那种存在，最终某种程度上也就真的成为了那种存在。

00:31:22.900 --> 00:31:24.700
And that's so great.
这感觉太棒了。

00:31:24.700 --> 00:31:28.779
Now we're sort of the age, almost the age of the, of future Ted.
现在我们差不多到了那个年龄，几乎就是未来泰德的年龄。

00:31:28.779 --> 00:31:34.460
And I still feel just about as confused as I ever did about life, but I feel, I feel a little older and a little wiser.
而且对于生活，我依然和以前一样困惑，但我感觉自己稍微年长了一些，也稍微更成熟了一点。

00:31:34.460 --> 00:31:46.460
And I think another big part of it was we were aware, we were, Carter and I, we were big thinkers, we're overthinkers, and we were very aware that a chapter of our lives had ended.
我想另一个重要原因是，我们意识到——我和卡特——我们都是很有深度思考的人，想得太多，而且我们非常清楚，我们人生中的一个章节已经结束了。

00:31:46.460 --> 00:31:47.460
The 20s in New York part had ended.
我们在纽约的二十多岁的那段时光结束了。

00:31:47.460 --> 00:32:00.220
We were writers for David Letterman, and we made this leap, and this will be a very heavy thing to bring up and talk about it, sitcom, but we, you know, 9-11 happened a little bit before, like that happened right before we moved to LA.
我们曾是大卫·莱特曼的编剧，然后我们做出了这个跨越，要聊情景喜剧可能会是一个很沉重的话题，但你知道的，9·11事件在那之前不久发生，就在我们搬到洛杉矶之前不久发生。

00:32:00.220 --> 00:32:09.819
And this is feeling in New York City sort of writ large that something had ended, some era had ended, something had changed that was not going to change back.
纽约市有一种普遍的感受，仿佛某种东西结束了，某个时代结束了，有什么事情已经改变，并且再也回不到过去了。

00:32:09.819 --> 00:32:13.539
And then we found ourselves living in Los Angeles trying to think of a pilot idea to write.
然后我们发现自己住在洛杉矶，绞尽脑汁想要构思一个试播集的创意。

00:32:13.539 --> 00:32:19.619
And we wrote about this nostalgia for our 20s in New York when we were like 30 and one day old.
我们写下了这份对我们在纽约二十多岁时的怀念，当时我们才30岁零一天。

00:32:19.619 --> 00:32:24.980
We did, we were in the nostalgic for our 20s in New York immediately.
真的，我们对在纽约的二十多岁时光产生了即时的怀念。

00:32:24.980 --> 00:32:29.859
And but nostalgic for like a time before this horrible tragedy hadn't happened, right?
而且是怀念那个可怕悲剧尚未发生之前的时代，对吧？

00:32:29.859 --> 00:33:02.480
We were still writing for Letterman when 9-11 happened, and then we left to move to LA the spring after that, like the spring of 2002, and I just think the sort of grabby tasks and the instant requirement to become more of a grown-up from living through 9-11 in New York and the instant nostalgia and the desire to sort of have things make sense or be back in some simpler time and have it a different time in your brain and your memories when things didn't feel the way they felt after none of them.
9·11发生时我们还在为莱特曼工作，然后在那之后的春天我们动身搬去了洛杉矶，就像2002年的春天，我只是觉得，经历纽约的9·11所带来的那种紧迫的任务，以及瞬间要求你变得更成熟的压力，还有那种即时的怀念，渴望让一切变得有意义，或者回到某个更简单的时代，在你的大脑和记忆中拥有一个不同的时代，当事情没有感觉像发生那些事之后的感受时。

00:33:02.480 --> 00:33:05.599
And I think that we'd be really informed this show.
我觉得这深深地影响了这部剧。

00:33:07.119 --> 00:33:13.400
It made us like maybe a little older than our years and it made us a little more reflective, a little more nostalgic.
它让我们显得比实际年龄稍微成熟了一点，也让我们变得更加深思熟虑、更加怀旧。

00:33:13.400 --> 00:33:21.079
And I think part of that is how we accessed writing from that future perspective even when we were in our 30s and were pretending to be the old wise guy.
我想部分原因正是我们如何从那个未来的视角进行创作，甚至在我们三十多岁、假装自己是老成持重的老者时也是如此。

00:33:21.079 --> 00:33:25.000
But somehow we could sort of fake our way into being that at least on paper.
但不知何故，我们至少在纸面上成功伪装成了那种人。

00:33:25.000 --> 00:33:32.079
And I sometimes people will quote months from future Ted to me and say that got them through a really hard time.
有时人们会把未来泰德的话引述给我，说那些话帮他们度过了非常艰难的时期。

00:33:32.079 --> 00:33:33.359
Thank you for those wise words.
谢谢你们那些充满智慧的话语。

00:33:33.359 --> 00:33:39.240
And I'm talking we were like 31 wishing we had that wisdom and sort of like play acting it.
而我说这些的时候，我们大概才31岁，正渴望拥有那种智慧，并有点像是在演戏。

00:33:39.240 --> 00:33:42.079
But it works somehow right?
但不知怎么的，它就是管用了，对吧？

00:33:42.079 --> 00:33:48.599
It feels to me like like Kenneth Branagh at 31 playing Lear and you're like, what are you doing?
在我看来，这有点像31岁的肯尼思·布拉纳在演李尔王，你会想：你在干什么？

00:33:50.200 --> 00:33:51.880
But also it's like he's good at it.
但同时又觉得：他演得真好。

00:33:51.880 --> 00:33:52.880
Right.
没错。

00:33:52.880 --> 00:33:56.279
You know, and it sort of, and does it, does it elevate you?
你知道的，而且它那种，它会提升你的境界吗？

00:33:56.279 --> 00:34:03.920
It's sort of that idea of like the fake until you make a thing, like it somehow it elevated us and made us feel like we really were that older, was it?
这就有点像“假装直到你成功”的理念，某种程度上它提升了我们的境界，让我们觉得自己真的成了那个成熟的长者，对吧？

00:34:03.920 --> 00:34:11.769
You know, speaking of, uh, of Jordan, who it seems like we're we talking about a bit, she has a thing which she'll sometimes say to her patients.
说到乔丹，感觉我们已经聊到她好几次了，她有一句经常对她的病人说的话。

00:34:11.769 --> 00:34:17.449
If someone's really confused, really tied in knots about something, she'll say, what is your 85 year old self say?
如果有人感到非常困惑、对某件事纠结不已，她会问：你85岁的自己会怎么说？

00:34:17.449 --> 00:34:18.449
She's going to like him in.
她会喜欢他的。

00:34:18.449 --> 00:34:19.449
She didn't like the show.
她不喜欢那个节目。

00:34:19.449 --> 00:34:20.449
Yeah.
是啊。

00:34:20.449 --> 00:34:20.992
question.
这个问题。

00:34:20.992 --> 00:34:26.473
And she said, if you ask yourself, what is my 85 year old self, the answer comes real fast.
她说，如果你问自己，“我85岁的自己会怎么想”，答案很快就会出现。

00:34:26.473 --> 00:34:27.798
And it's real service.
而且真的很有帮助。

00:34:27.798 --> 00:34:39.964
And I think, you know, I mentioned this in that piece I wrote about Ted for my muse letters, but Dick Schwartz has this thing, internal family systems, IFS, which is like really, it's called parts work.
我觉得，你知道的，我在为我的缪斯信件写的那篇关于泰德的文章中提到过这一点，迪克·施瓦茨有一个理论，叫做内部家庭系统（IFS），这其实就是所谓的“部分工作法”。

00:34:39.964 --> 00:34:46.650
And you're locating these parts of yourself that are sometimes at odds with each other, but they're all in you.
你要找出自己内心深处那些有时彼此冲突、但都存在于你之中的不同部分。

00:34:46.650 --> 00:34:51.769
And it strikes me that you and Carter like, like, and me too, we have an elder in us.
这让我觉得，你和卡特——还有我——我们的内心里都住着一位长者。

00:34:51.769 --> 00:35:05.550
We have an older, I don't know if it's ancestral or just a wise old, something connected more towards the bigger picture, the perennial kind of wisdom, and we can go there.
我们内心都有一个更年长的人，我不知道这是一种祖先的传承，还是一种明智的老成，某种与宏大视角、永恒智慧更加紧密相连的东西，我们可以触及它。

00:35:05.550 --> 00:35:10.650
We can access it at 31 or 30 whenever you guys were writing.
我们在31岁或者你们创作时无论几岁的年纪，都能调用这种智慧。

00:35:10.650 --> 00:35:20.667
The other thing that, I don't know if it was you or Carter, but first season, what year was older Ted narrating 2030?
另一件事是，我不知道是你是卡特，但在第一季里，年长的泰德是在哪一年旁白？2030年吗？

00:35:20.667 --> 00:35:21.609
2030, yeah.
2030年，对。

00:35:21.609 --> 00:35:22.109
2030.
2030年。

